00:00:02.480 --> 00:00:07.110
So in uh previously uh I've discussed uh

00:00:07.120 --> 00:00:10.310
sequential logic um and in particular

00:00:10.320 --> 00:00:12.310
I've looked at what happens if we try to

00:00:12.320 --> 00:00:15.509
design asynchronous sequential logic and

00:00:15.519 --> 00:00:18.710
the timing problems that arise. In this

00:00:18.720 --> 00:00:21.189
uh video, part of my series and the

00:00:21.199 --> 00:00:22.790
introduction to digital design using

00:00:22.800 --> 00:00:26.710
VHDL, I'm going to look at the design of

00:00:26.720 --> 00:00:28.470
synchronous sequential logic and how

00:00:28.480 --> 00:00:32.310
we'd implement it in hardware.

00:00:32.320 --> 00:00:34.389
So essentially, so why do we need it?

00:00:34.399 --> 00:00:35.670
The timing problems that arise in

00:00:35.680 --> 00:00:37.910
asynchronous logic can be reduced by

00:00:37.920 --> 00:00:39.750
introducing some sort of synchronizing

00:00:39.760 --> 00:00:42.790
circuit which allows the logic to switch

00:00:42.800 --> 00:00:45.430
once only in a clock period. So by

00:00:45.440 --> 00:00:47.110
synchronous we mean we're using a clock

00:00:47.120 --> 00:00:49.750
to synchronize everything. These are

00:00:49.760 --> 00:00:52.150
known as clocked sequential circuits. And

00:00:52.160 --> 00:00:53.910
although they're not as fast as

00:00:53.920 --> 00:00:56.630
asynchronous circuits, clearly we now

00:00:56.640 --> 00:00:58.150
rather than have outputs change

00:00:58.160 --> 00:01:00.869
immediately or after a short gate delay,

00:01:00.879 --> 00:01:02.630
we now have to wait for the next clock

00:01:02.640 --> 00:01:04.869
edge. They're much easier to design and

00:01:04.879 --> 00:01:06.630
implement.

00:01:06.640 --> 00:01:09.510
So syn synchronous logic is a almost

00:01:09.520 --> 00:01:13.350
always a more appropriate solution for

00:01:13.360 --> 00:01:16.870
um and that includes in in almost well

00:01:16.880 --> 00:01:19.030
most or I would say almost all uh

00:01:19.040 --> 00:01:21.350
programmable systems.

00:01:21.360 --> 00:01:24.710
So the basic principle is for our

00:01:24.720 --> 00:01:27.109
synchronous sequential logic as before

00:01:27.119 --> 00:01:29.670
we have some external inputs potentially

00:01:29.680 --> 00:01:31.510
we have some com a combinational logic

00:01:31.520 --> 00:01:34.710
circuit and this provides what's called

00:01:34.720 --> 00:01:36.870
the next state excitation variable. So

00:01:36.880 --> 00:01:38.469
the output of this combinational logic

00:01:38.479 --> 00:01:41.270
is going to be the next state in our our

00:01:41.280 --> 00:01:42.789
logic.

00:01:42.799 --> 00:01:45.270
Now remember that we had these idealized

00:01:45.280 --> 00:01:47.350
circuit delays. And the idea the idea of

00:01:47.360 --> 00:01:49.510
synchronous is we add in a synchronizing

00:01:49.520 --> 00:01:51.990
signal so that the output state only

00:01:52.000 --> 00:01:55.109
changes once all of those uh next state

00:01:55.119 --> 00:01:56.789
values have settled all the glitches

00:01:56.799 --> 00:01:58.709
have finished or we've got there no

00:01:58.719 --> 00:02:01.429
hazards left. So we introduce that to

00:02:01.439 --> 00:02:04.709
control when the transitions occur. the

00:02:04.719 --> 00:02:06.950
current state's updated uh to the next

00:02:06.960 --> 00:02:08.229
state on the occurrence of the

00:02:08.239 --> 00:02:11.270
synchronizing signal and the new output

00:02:11.280 --> 00:02:13.030
only also becomes available in the

00:02:13.040 --> 00:02:16.229
occurrence of the signal.

00:02:16.239 --> 00:02:18.070
So what kind of element can we use for

00:02:18.080 --> 00:02:19.670
that? Well, basically we can use a

00:02:19.680 --> 00:02:21.270
flip-flop. Either an edge triggered

00:02:21.280 --> 00:02:23.830
D-type flip-flop or a JK flip-flop could

00:02:23.840 --> 00:02:26.470
be used both triggered on an edge.

00:02:26.480 --> 00:02:27.990
Synchronizing signal is therefore a

00:02:28.000 --> 00:02:30.470
periodic pulse train. So we have a clock

00:02:30.480 --> 00:02:32.630
sequence periodic pulse train which

00:02:32.640 --> 00:02:35.670
controls the uh which enables the inputs

00:02:35.680 --> 00:02:37.990
to some sort of memory element as I said

00:02:38.000 --> 00:02:40.630
D-type or JK type flip-flop usually

00:02:40.640 --> 00:02:42.309
it's a set of memory elements because we

00:02:42.319 --> 00:02:45.750
have one bit uh for each of the bits

00:02:45.760 --> 00:02:49.910
that that correspond to the next state.

